# AsynCodeBench

<div align="center">

**Dependency-centric evaluation of asynchronous multi-agent coding**

[![CI](https://github.com/KaituoZhang/AsynCodeBench/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg?branch=main)](https://github.com/KaituoZhang/AsynCodeBench/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![Release](https://img.shields.io/badge/release-v0.4.1-orange.svg)](manifests/release/v0.4/official_tasks.json)
[![Tasks](https://img.shields.io/badge/tasks-19-4C78A8.svg)](manifests/release/v0.4/task_index.json)
[![Protocols](https://img.shields.io/badge/protocols-5-59A14F.svg)](configs/evaluation/protocol_registry.v2.json)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-Apache--2.0-blue.svg)](LICENSE)

[Website](https://asyncodebench.org) · [Quickstart](docs/QUICKSTART.md) · [Documentation](docs/README.md) · [Specification](SPECIFICATION_v0.3.md)

</div>

AsynCodeBench is a community-ready benchmark for studying how coding agents
produce, communicate, and integrate dependent changes. It evaluates the same
19 repository-level tasks under five controlled single- and multi-agent
protocols, with checkpointed dependency probes that expose *when* and *where*
collaboration succeeds or breaks.

> **Release status:** v0.4.1 community preview. The task set, protocol
> contracts, execution budget, and result-validity rules are frozen for
> comparable runs.

## What It Measures

| Dimension | Measurement |
| --- | --- |
| Broad implementation progress | Test Pass Rate |
| Final producer–consumer closure | Async Dependency Pass Rate (ADPR) |
| When dependencies first resolve | Dependency Resolution Step (DRS) |
| End-to-end completion | Final Success Rate (FSR) |
| Resource use | Model token consumption |

Unlike a final-test-only benchmark, AsynCodeBench records dependency state
throughout execution. This separates individual coding ability from the
ability to coordinate changes across agents and files.

## Quick Start

**Requirements:** Linux `x86_64`, Git, Docker, [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/),
Python 3.12, and an OpenAI-compatible model endpoint.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/KaituoZhang/AsynCodeBench.git
cd AsynCodeBench
bash scripts/setup_evaluation.sh
```

Configure the generated runner environment:

```bash
cd reproductions/async-swe-agents
$EDITOR .env
```

At minimum, set `LLM_BASE_URL`, `LLM_API_KEY`, and a LiteLLM-qualified
`LLM_MODEL`, such as `openai/your-model-name`. Then verify the endpoint and
inspect the official release:

```bash
export ENV_FILE="$PWD/.env"
source scripts/env.sh

uv run asyncodebench doctor
uv run asyncodebench release-status --require preview
uv run asyncodebench tasks
```

Run all five protocols on one task:

```bash
ENV_FILE="$PWD/.env" \
MODEL_TAG=my-model \
RUN_VERSION=official-v01 \
WORKSPACE_PORT_STRATEGY=auto \
scripts/run_asyncodebench_five_protocols_env.sh cachetools
```

For model configuration, local vLLM serving, compiler tasks, campaign runs,
and failure handling, follow the full [Quickstart](docs/QUICKSTART.md) and
[Model Experiment Runbook](docs/MODEL_EXPERIMENT_RUNBOOK.md).

## Five Controlled Protocols

| Protocol | Execution model |
| --- | --- |
| `single` | One iterative agent owns the complete task. |
| `serial_specialists` | Specialists execute in dependency order with merged upstream handoffs. |
| `async_private` | Specialists work concurrently from the same base in private worktrees. |
| **Async-Manager-RO** (`caid_manager` in the CLI) | A read-only manager delegates, reviews, and integrates specialist artifacts. |
| `async_manager` | An online manager observes checkpoints and may intervene under recorded, scope-validated controls. |

Every official condition uses the same manifest-defined task contract and the
same 100-response capability budget.

## Repository Map

```text
configs/       execution profiles, task sources, and protocol registry
manifests/     frozen task, scenario, dependency, and release records
reproductions/ executable five-protocol agent harness
src/           task-construction and dependency-analysis library
schemas/       machine-readable benchmark contracts
scripts/       setup, validation, image, and analysis utilities
tests/         contract, unit, integration, and smoke tests
docs/          methodology, runbooks, metrics, and validity rules
skills/        optional workflow for contributing candidate tasks
```

Generated trajectories and reports are written to:

```text
reproductions/async-swe-agents/outputs/<suite>/<version>/<model>/<task>/<protocol>/<run-id>/
```

Validate a completed bundle without calling the model again:

```bash
uv run asyncodebench validate-run outputs/<suite>/<version>/<model>/<task>/<protocol>/<run-id>
```

See [Repository Layout](docs/REPOSITORY_LAYOUT.md) for the maintained-source and
generated-artifact boundaries.

